.TH PKM 1 "2026-04-21" "pkm 0.1.0" "InterGenOS System Administration"
.SH NAME
pkm \- InterGenOS package manager
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B pkm
[\fB\-\-version\fR]
[\fB\-\-db\fR \fIPATH\fR]
[\fB\-\-root\fR \fIDIR\fR]
.I command
[\fIargs\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B pkm
is the InterGenOS package manager. It installs, removes, queries, and
verifies packages built from InterGenOS's tiered source tree. Packages
are distributed as
.B .igos.tar.gz
archives with accompanying metadata and are tracked in a local SQLite
database at
.IR /var/lib/igos/pkm.db .
.PP
Repository configuration lives in
.IR /etc/pkm/repos.conf .
The default shipped repository is the official InterGenOS mirror. GPG
verification becomes available once the signing-key ceremony completes;
until then the trust model is TLS + repository-URL pinning.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.BR install " \fIPACKAGE\fR [\fIPACKAGE...\fR] [\fB\-\-archive\fR \fIPATH\fR]"
Install one or more packages. If no local archive is supplied, packages
are fetched from configured repositories and dependencies are resolved
automatically.
.TP
.BR install-helper " \fIPACKAGE\fR"
Install proprietary or third-party software via a named download helper
(for example,
.BR chrome ,
.BR vscode ,
.BR claude-code ).
Helpers are installed at
.IR /usr/bin/igos-install-* .
This exists so the user-facing interface is one command regardless of
whether a package is source-built or vendor-supplied.
.TP
.BR remove " \fIPACKAGE\fR [\fB\-\-force\fR]"
Remove an installed package.
.B \-\-force
permits removal even when other packages still depend on it.
.TP
.BR list " [" installed | available | upgradable "] [" \-\-tier " \fITIER\fR]"
List packages. The default view is
.BR installed .
Restrict to a tier with
.B \-\-tier
.RI ( toolchain ,
.IR core ,
.IR base ,
.IR desktop ,
.IR extra ,
.IR ai ).
.TP
.B update
Sync package indexes from every enabled repository.
.TP
.BR upgrade " [\fIPACKAGE...\fR] [\fB\-\-all\fR] [\fB\-\-archive\fR \fIPATH\fR [\fB\-\-archive\-trust\fR \fIMODE\fR]]"
Upgrade installed packages to the latest versions available in the
configured repositories. A bare
.B upgrade
refuses to act: name the packages, or pass
.B \-\-all
to consider every installed package (a plan summary and a confirmation
prompt follow). With
.BR \-\-archive ,
exactly one named, installed package is moved forward from a local archive
instead of the repository: the archive's own metadata must name that
package, an older build refuses unless
.B \-\-allow\-downgrade
is given, every dependency the archive declares must already be installed,
and the trust modes are those of
.BR "install \-\-archive"
(\fIstrict\fR, the default, requires the signed index to carry the exact
archive; \fIloose\fR proceeds with a warning, which a build the mirror does
not serve yet needs). The restore point, the rollback copy and the
configuration-file protection of a repository upgrade apply to both.
When
.B \-\-all
finds the package manager itself among the upgradable packages, pkm upgrades
FIRST and the command then re-executes itself under the new release for the
rest of the queue (it says so before the confirmation prompt, which covers
both halves), so the remaining packages are always installed by the code the
new pkm ships, never by the code being replaced.
.TP
.BR restart\-services " [\fB\-\-list\fR | \fB\-\-all\fR | \fIUNIT...\fR]"
Restart services that are still running old code after an upgrade. With
.BR \-\-list ,
classify the packages installed or upgraded since THIS boot (a package
installed before the boot was loaded fresh at boot and is counted, not
listed) and print the ones that need a restart or a reboot; read-only. With
.BR \-\-all ,
restart every running unit that belongs to a package upgraded since this boot
and that started before that upgrade (systemd's active-since stamp is compared
with the upgrade's history stamp; a unit already restarted after the upgrade
is reported as current and left alone). The units that carry the login
session (the system bus, logind, the journal, the display manager) are never
restarted by
.BR \-\-all ;
a package whose only running units are such is reported as REBOOT REQUIRED
instead, because a live restart would end the session. Packages that declare
a reboot are reported only when they changed since this boot.
.B \-\-all
refuses to act when the boot time cannot be read from /proc/stat. Naming a
unit restarts exactly that unit, with a warning first if it carries the
session. Exit status 0 on success, 1 if any restart failed or
.B \-\-all
could not bound itself to this boot.
.TP
.BR search " \fITERM\fR"
Search local and remote indexes for packages matching
.IR TERM .
Installed matches are labelled inline.
.TP
.BR info " \fIPACKAGE\fR"
Show package details: version, tier, description, license, build and
install dates, install method, dependencies, and reverse dependencies.
For an available but uninstalled package, show the cached repository metadata
and state that it is not installed.
Exit status is 0 when the package is registered as installed, or 1 when it is
not installed, including when available metadata is printed. An unknown name
or unreadable index retains the plain not-installed message.
This status describes package registration, not file integrity or completion
of a vendor download. The alias
.B show
has the same behavior.
.TP
.BR files " \fIPACKAGE\fR"
List files owned by an installed package.
.TP
.BR provides " \fIFILE\fR"
Report which installed package owns a given file.
.TP
.BR verify " [\fIPACKAGE\fR] [\fB\-\-all\fR]"
Verify package integrity against recorded file hashes. With
.B \-\-all
every installed package is verified and a summary is printed.
Exit status for a named package is 0 when every recorded file was checked and
passed, 1 when a file is missing or modified or a critical install hook left
the package degraded, 3 when nothing failed but a check could not run (most
often files the invoking user may not read), and 4 when the package is
not installed — nothing was verified, so the status does not report success.
A missing package name without
.B \-\-all
is a usage error and exits 2.
.TP
.BR depends " \fIPACKAGE\fR [\fB\-\-reverse\fR]"
Show the dependencies of a package. With
.B \-\-reverse
show which installed packages depend on it.
.TP
.BR history " [\fIPACKAGE\fR] [\fB\-\-limit\fR \fIN\fR | \fB\-\-all\fR]"
Show operation history (install/remove/upgrade). An optional
.I PACKAGE
argument filters to a single package. Show the 50 most recent matching
entries by default, newest first. Use
.B \-\-limit N
to select a positive number of entries, or
.B \-\-all
to show every matching entry. These options are mutually exclusive.
.TP
.B import
Import pre-existing text-manifest records into the SQLite database.
Used when migrating from the legacy pkm text-manifest format, and run by the
build pipeline after each package is installed.
.IP
A manifest may carry
.B HOOK\-MANAGED:
header lines naming files whose live content the package's own sealed
lifecycle hook created or rewrote. Those files are registered as hook-managed
content: existence is checked, the byte comparison against the recorded
payload hash is not, and they are listed in their own bucket by
.BR "pkm verify" .
A manifest may only name files it also lists in its own FILE LIST, and naming
a file never reaches the ownership record another package holds for the same
path. Every rejected claim is printed, naming the manifest, the package and
the path.
.TP
.BR hook\-baseline " \fIPACKAGE\fR \fB\-\-out\fR \fIFILE\fR"
Record the current sha256 of every regular file
.I PACKAGE
owns, for comparison after its
.B post_install
has run. Used by the build system, which runs a recipe's
.B post_install
after the package's records are already written from the pristine staged
files. Reads only; changes nothing.
.TP
.BR record\-hook\-changes " \fIPACKAGE\fR \fB\-\-baseline\fR \fIFILE\fR"
Compare
.IR PACKAGE 's
own files against a baseline written by
.B hook\-baseline
before its
.B post_install
ran. A file whose content changed across that window is recorded as
hook-managed: existence is checked, the byte comparison against the payload
hash is not, and the text manifest is re-emitted stating the class so the
record survives a later
.BR import .
Only files
.I PACKAGE
owns are considered, and a file that merely disagrees with its recorded
hash is never reclassified \(em a change must be observed across the window,
because inferring one from a disagreement would stop checking a damaged
file.
.TP
.BR vacuum " [\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR]"
Return unused space inside the package database to the filesystem.
Removing packages and collapsing duplicated records leave free pages
inside the database file; the database reuses them but does not hand them
back until the file is rebuilt.
.B vacuum
rebuilds it into a fresh file and swaps that in.
.IP
Nothing runs this on its own. A maintenance step that frees a large number
of records prints one line saying how much is now reclaimable; when to pay
for the rebuild is the user's decision, because the rebuild rewrites the
whole file and needs free disk space of roughly twice its current size
while the old and new copies both exist.
.IP
That space is checked first and the command
.B refuses
rather than starting a rebuild that could run out of disk partway, which
would leave a full filesystem and no compaction. No installed file is
touched and no package record changes.
.B \-\-dry\-run
reports how much would be returned and whether there is room to do it,
then exits without changing anything.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print version and exit.
.TP
.BR \-\-db " \fIPATH\fR"
Override the default database path
.RI ( /var/lib/igos/pkm.db ).
Primarily useful in tests and rescue scenarios. An explicit
.B \-\-db
wins over the path
.B \-\-root
would derive.
.TP
.BR \-\-root " \fIDIR\fR"
Install into
.I DIR
instead of this system. Every file the operation writes, and every piece of
pkm's own state, lives under
.IR DIR :
the packages themselves, the package database, the text manifests, the archive
directory, the helper footprint manifests, the mutation lock, the repository
cache, the available\-updates report, the anti\-rollback record, the
per\-package hook directory and the pre\-transaction handler directory. Nothing
outside
.I DIR
is written.
.IP
.I DIR
must already exist and must be an absolute path; pkm creates its own state
directories underneath an install root but never the root itself, so a
mistyped path is refused rather than silently becoming a new empty system.
.IP
Two things are deliberately NOT taken from
.IR DIR :
the repository configuration
.RI ( /etc/pkm/repos.conf )
and the keyring used to verify signatures
.RI ( /etc/pkm/trusted.gpg )
are read from the running system. A target being built has no keyring of its
own to verify its packages against, and a target that had one would be
deciding which signatures pkm accepts while pkm installs into it. Where
packages are put is what this option controls; which signatures pkm believes
is a property of the machine doing the installing.
.IP
A subcommand that cannot be honoured under another root is refused by name,
with the reason, before anything is opened or changed \- rather than doing the
part it can and staying quiet about the part it cannot. The commands that can
be run under
.B \-\-root
are
.BR install ,
.BR remove ,
.BR verify ,
.BR import ,
.BR update ,
.BR cache ,
.BR vacuum ,
.BR hold ,
.BR unhold ,
.BR mark ,
and the read\-only queries
.RB ( list ,
.BR search ,
.BR info ,
.BR files ,
.BR provides ,
.BR depends ,
.BR history ).
.IP
Two further refusals belong to
.BR install .
A proprietary\-download package is refused for another root: it is installed by
running the vendor's own installer with a person accepting the vendor's license
at this machine's keyboard, which cannot be performed on behalf of a different
filesystem. And a package whose own post\-install hook could not run inside
.I DIR
is refused before anything is deployed \- pkm runs such a hook inside the root
it installs into, so a root with no interpreter for it would take the install
to a reported success with the package's post\-install step never having run.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B PKM_PRETXN_HANDLER_DIR
Override the pre-transaction handler directory with an absolute path.
When unset, commands use the selected install root's registered handlers.
An empty or relative value is rejected.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/pkm/repos.conf
Repository configuration. Each stanza defines a named repository with a
base URL and optional trust settings.
.TP
.I /var/lib/igos/pkm.db
SQLite database tracking installed packages, files, and history. Self-
initialises on first invocation.
.TP
.I /var/lib/igos/packages/
Per-package metadata directories.
.TP
.I /var/lib/igos/archives/
Cached
.B .igos.tar.gz
archives.
.TP
.I /var/lib/pkm/state/
The anti\-rollback record: the newest index generation this system has
accepted. An index older than the recorded one is refused.
.PP
Under
.BR \-\-root " \fIDIR\fR"
every path in this section is read and written under
.I DIR
instead, with the two exceptions named in
.BR OPTIONS .
.SH EXAMPLES
Install into a directory rather than into this system \- the package, its
database record, its manifest and its caches all land under the directory, and
nothing outside it is written:
.PP
.RS
.B pkm \-\-root /mnt/target install font\-alias
.RE
.PP
Query what that root holds, without touching this system's database:
.PP
.RS
.B pkm \-\-root /mnt/target list
.RE
.PP
Install a single package from the default repository:
.PP
.RS
.B pkm install htop
.RE
.PP
Install several packages in one operation; dependencies resolve
automatically:
.PP
.RS
.B pkm install vim tmux ripgrep
.RE
.PP
Install from a local archive when offline:
.PP
.RS
.B pkm install vim \-\-archive /path/to/vim-9.1.1.igos.tar.gz
.PP
Move an installed package forward from a build the mirror does not serve yet:
.PP
.B pkm upgrade forge \-\-archive /path/to/forge-1.0.igos.tar.gz \-\-archive\-trust loose
.RE
.PP
Install proprietary software via a download helper:
.PP
.RS
.B pkm install-helper vscode
.RE
.PP
Show what a package installed, along with its version and tier:
.PP
.RS
.B pkm info htop
.RE
.PP
List every file owned by a package:
.PP
.RS
.B pkm files htop
.RE
.PP
Find the owning package for a file on disk:
.PP
.RS
.B pkm provides /usr/bin/nano
.RE
.PP
Verify the integrity of every installed package:
.PP
.RS
.B pkm verify \-\-all
.RE
.PP
After an upgrade, see which services still run old code, then restart them
(the login session is never touched):
.PP
.RS
.B pkm restart\-services \-\-list
.br
.B sudo pkm restart\-services \-\-all
.RE
.PP
List everything in the desktop tier:
.PP
.RS
.B pkm list installed \-\-tier desktop
.RE
